# Security headers for the visitor app.
#
# Everything Apache serves out of this directory - index.html, the service
# worker, the scanner and model bundles - is static, so the request never
# enters Laravel and App\Http\Middleware\SecurityHeaders never runs. That is
# why the staff panel carried a Content-Security-Policy and the half of the
# system that opens a camera carried none.
#
# This is a directory-level file rather than an <If> block in ../.htaccess
# for two reasons: the directory is exactly the scope wanted, and <If> needs
# AllowOverride All where these directives need only FileInfo.
#
# ../.htaccess still supplies X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options and
# Referrer-Policy; those merge down and are not repeated here.

<IfModule mod_headers.c>

    # This policy is deliberately narrower than the panel's: the visitor app
    # loads no CDN scripts at all, only local bundles under /js/vendor/.
    #
    # 'unsafe-inline' for script-src is not a shortcut and must not be
    # dropped on its own: index.html carries 174 inline on* handlers and an
    # inline <script>, and index.php - the entry point the printed QR codes
    # point at - is an inline script and nothing else. Removing it without
    # first migrating those to addEventListener does not harden the app, it
    # switches it off.
    #
    # 'unsafe-eval' is required, and leaving it out switched image
    # recognition off in production while every local check still passed.
    #
    # The claim this comment used to make - that the local bundles contain no
    # eval, pinned by SecurityHeadersTest - was only ever true of
    # teachablemachine-image.min.js, which is the only file that test reads.
    # tf.min.js was never checked, and it carries a regenerator-runtime shim
    # that builds `Function("return this")` during tmImage.load(). Under a
    # policy without 'unsafe-eval' that throws an EvalError, _loadModel()
    # swallows it, and the app drops to the pHash fallback - the visitor sees
    # "Photo Match" and "no exhibit recognized" with nothing in any server
    # log, and nothing in the console unless someone thinks to open it.
    #
    # Laragon does apply this file - http://localhost/visitor/sw.js answers
    # with the policy below - so a laptop can reproduce this. What hides it
    # locally is the worker, not Apache: see the note on connect-src.
    #
    # The staff trainer at /recognition already allows eval for the same
    # library, through App\Http\Middleware\SecurityHeaders. This is the half
    # of the system that actually runs the model in the room.
    #
    # storage.googleapis.com is where teachablemachine-image fetches the
    # MobileNet base if the copy in /visitor/model/ does not load.
    #
    # There are no Google Fonts hosts here, in any directive, and that is not
    # an oversight - the visitor app serves its own faces out of
    # /visitor/fonts/ now. See public/visitor/css/fonts.css.
    #
    # They used to be needed in style-src and font-src, and the omission of
    # connect-src is what broke the app: sw.js routes every cross-origin GET
    # through staleWhileRevalidate, which re-issues it as fetch(), and a
    # fetch() from inside a worker answers to connect-src no matter what the
    # tag that first asked for it was allowed to do. Both stylesheets were
    # refused there, the catch swallowed it, and the <link> got back
    # Response.error(). The icon font failing is the loud half: a Material
    # Icons span holds the glyph name as its text, so visitors read "wifi"
    # and "login" where the icons belong. Young Serif and Instrument Sans
    # failed in the same breath and only fell back to system faces, which is
    # why nobody caught it sooner.
    #
    # Widening connect-src would have fixed it, but slowly and twice over: a
    # service worker keeps the policy its own script response carried when it
    # was installed, for as long as it stays the active worker, and the
    # browser only installs a replacement if sw.js differs byte for byte - a
    # change made in a header alone gives it no reason to. Same-origin files
    # need none of that. connect-src 'self' already covers them, so the fix
    # works under whatever policy a given device's worker is still holding.
    #
    # blob: appears twice on purpose: img-src for frames grabbed off the
    # camera, media-src for html5-qrcode's older-browser path, which attaches
    # the stream to the <video> through a blob: URL.
    #
    # Nothing here allows static.cloudflareinsights.com. Cloudflare injects
    # that beacon at the edge when Web Analytics automatic setup is on, and
    # this policy refuses it. The fix is the Cloudflare dashboard, not this
    # line: the app does not ask for the beacon and should not have to name
    # a third-party script host to stay quiet.
    Header set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' https://storage.googleapis.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none';"

    # The QR scanner needs the camera and the museum-info screen needs the
    # position. (self) and not (*): only a page from this origin may ask, and
    # the browser still puts the question to the visitor.
    Header set Permissions-Policy "camera=(self), geolocation=(self), microphone=(), payment=()"

</IfModule>
